{"w":2400,"h":3395,"lines":[{"c":0.30000001192092896,"x":0.7716049391467531,"t":"1718867188","w":0.069958845774332645,"y":0.046511627815394085,"h":0.008720930389296444},{"y":0.053779069625663367,"c":1,"x":0.1172839502042468,"t":"Test 3","w":0.057613169352213542,"h":0.013081395583944611},{"t":"Chapter four focuses on immigration and the location of neighborhoods and tenements in the","x":0.11728394737907706,"y":0.097383720783960293,"w":0.74279836018880208,"h":0.015988372380376759,"c":1},{"c":1,"w":0.75102879842122405,"t":"late 19th century. Barr identifies four primary immigrant enclaves and analyzes their locations","x":0.11728395459156322,"y":0.11482558126265718,"h":0.01453488398216074},{"x":0.11722444407287942,"t":"in terms of the amenities available in the area. Most of these enclaves were located on the least","y":0.1298767407043937,"h":0.015329404770274779,"c":1,"w":0.75527018229166665},{"c":1,"y":0.14811542991499604,"t":"valuable land, between the industries located on the waterfront and the wealthy neighborhoods","w":0.75308639526367194,"x":0.11522634960452015,"h":0.016195524146054185},{"y":0.16569767427982374,"t":"bordering Central Park.","h":0.01453488398216074,"w":0.19341564178466797,"c":1,"x":0.11522633537991828},{"y":0.19758539482793558,"t":"Part two of the book begins with a discussion of the economics of skyscraper height. In chapter","x":0.11522633953751722,"w":0.7633744812011718,"h":0.01472320376914027,"c":1},{"y":0.21366279055165327,"c":1,"x":0.11522634424963106,"w":0.73251027425130211,"h":0.016019188192342826,"t":"five, Barr distinguishes between engineering height, economic height, and developer height"},{"x":0.11522634584652565,"y":0.23110465101644029,"c":1,"h":0.01598837238037687,"w":0.77777776082356764,"t":"where engineering height is the tallest building that can be safely made at a given time, economic"},{"t":"height is the height that is most efficient from society's point of view, and developer height is the","x":0.11522634676171691,"y":0.24705535915217913,"c":1,"w":0.77160491943359366,"h":0.016026036337772664},{"h":0.016195524146054296,"y":0.26442873984771986,"x":0.11522632697870004,"c":1,"t":"actual height chosen by the developer, who is attempting to maximize return on investment.","w":0.73456792195638021},{"w":0.72222223917643225,"h":0.01598837238037687,"c":1,"y":0.29651162776057149,"t":"Chapter five also has an interesting discussion of the technological advances that led to the","x":0.11728394204010727},{"c":1,"t":"construction of skyscrapers. For example, the introduction of iron and steel skeletal frames made","x":0.115144636259276,"y":0.31121930499728467,"w":0.77382522583007829,"h":0.018449026788502576},{"y":0.32979976452883952,"x":0.11522632708369657,"t":"thick, load-bearing walls unnecessary, expanding the usable square footage of buildings and","w":0.7345679219563801,"h":0.016195524146054185,"c":1},{"t":"increasing the use of windows and availability of natural light. Chapter six then presents data on","h":0.01453488398216074,"c":1,"y":0.34738372079724233,"w":0.76954732259114567,"x":0.11522633860759821},{"x":0.11522634256699604,"y":0.36336866908085419,"t":"building height throughout the 20th century and uses regression analysis to 'predict' building","w":0.74485595703125007,"h":0.015991796172268979,"c":1},{"t":"construction. While less technical than the research paper on which the chapter is based, it is","y":0.37928004774881341,"c":1,"w":0.73878097534179688,"x":0.11723545188088109,"h":0.016330260129643337},{"c":1,"t":"probably more technical than would be preferred by a general audience.","x":0.11522633420307102,"y":0.3968023254344557,"w":0.57613169352213534,"h":0.016036308275094724},{"y":0.43021201413222843,"c":1,"w":0.74279836018880219,"t":"Chapter seven tackles theibedrock myth', the assumption that the absence of bedrock close to the","h":0.014555427856804859,"x":0.11728394737299586},{"w":0.73456792195638021,"c":1,"t":"surface between Downtown and Midtown New York is the reason for skyscrapers not being built","x":0.11522632699305424,"h":0.014723203769140158,"y":0.446113074476989},{"h":0.016740788278927998,"c":1,"w":0.78197392781575514,"t":"between the two urban centers. Rather, Barr argues that while deeper bedrock does increase foundation","x":0.11518711015618407,"y":0.46215784849072561},{"h":0.015988372380376759,"x":0.11522634335246029,"w":0.73868311564127609,"t":"costs, these costs were neither prohibitively high nor were they large compared to the overall cost","y":0.47965116264199315,"c":1},{"t":"of building a skyscraper. What I enjoyed the most about this chapter was Barr's discussion of how","y":0.49558303880142851,"w":0.74279836018880196,"h":0.016044868316470673,"c":1,"x":0.115226334195625},{"t":"foundations are actually built. He describes the use of caissons, which enable workers to dig down","y":0.51295641949739423,"x":0.1152263423649859,"w":0.74485595703125018,"h":0.016195524146054185,"c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.52906976729571631,"x":0.11522634426247577,"t":"for considerable distances, often below the water table, until they reach bedrock. Barr's thorough","w":0.73251027425130211,"h":0.015988372380376759},{"h":0.017538979421375167,"t":"technological history discusses not only how caissons work, but also the dangers involved. While this","c":1,"w":0.76965011596679689,"y":0.54483598175058268,"x":0.11518073438923353},{"h":0.016195524146054185,"w":0.54732508341471364,"y":0.56242638383437893,"c":1,"x":0.11522634707865137,"t":"chapter references empirical research papers, it is a relatively easy read."},{"y":0.59593023242542387,"w":0.73045267740885422,"t":"Chapters eight and nine focus on the birth of Midtown and the building boom of the 1920s.","c":1,"h":0.01453488398216074,"x":0.11728394925585973},{"x":0.1193415661478187,"w":0.70576131184895841,"h":0.014557139752750747,"c":1,"t":"Chapter eight contains lengthy discussions of urban economic theory that may serve as a","y":0.61189634874685173},{"x":0.11514803489920382,"t":"distraction to readers primarily interested in New York. However, they would be well-suited for","w":0.75940734863281256,"h":0.018963226300386715,"c":1,"y":0.62712772615557721},{"t":"undergraduates learning about the economics of cities. In the next chapter, Barr considers two of","w":0.77572016398111976,"c":1,"y":0.64534883706343538,"x":0.11522633766246448,"h":0.016017476296396826},{"t":"the primary explanations for the building boom of the 1920s - the first being exuberance, and","y":0.66133720915515781,"x":0.11522634054689623,"w":0.75720163981119781,"h":0.01598837238037687,"c":1},{"w":0.74074076334635419,"t":"the second being financing. He uses data to assess the viability of these two explanations and","h":0.014574259835502756,"x":0.11728393922345504,"y":0.67873969378122057,"c":1},{"t":"finds that supply and demand factors explain much of the development of the 1920s; though it","x":0.11522634876734097,"y":0.69464075391553193,"c":1,"h":0.016195524146054185,"w":0.75308639526367194},{"w":0.5617284138997396,"c":1,"y":0.71220930217811584,"h":0.016000356213644928,"t":"enabled the boom, cheap credit was not, he argues, the primary cause.","x":0.11522632791009552},{"y":0.7441107184454725,"t":"In the final chapter (chapter 10), Barr discusses another of his empirical papers that estimates","w":0.74691360473632806,"c":1,"h":0.016195524146054185,"x":0.11522632509998251},{"w":0.77777776082356753,"y":0.76148409915996229,"h":0.014723203769140158,"c":1,"x":0.11728395898951334,"t":"Manhattan land values from the mid-19th century to the present day. The data work that went into"},{"t":"these estimations is particularly impressive. Toward the end of the chapter, Barr assesses 'whether","w":0.78395060221354163,"c":1,"y":0.777616278919538,"h":0.01598837238037687,"x":0.11522634504363737},{"x":0.11728393925651631,"y":0.79505300372642107,"t":"skyscrapers are a cause or an effect of high land values'. He finds that changes in land values","w":0.74074076334635408,"h":0.013086531271782831,"c":1},{"w":0.73868311564127609,"c":1,"h":0.016195524146054185,"y":0.81095406348391086,"t":"predict future building height, but the reverse is not true. The book ends with an epilogue, in","x":0.11522634333283993},{"c":1,"y":0.82848837197356806,"w":0.7633744812011718,"x":0.11934156588418526,"h":0.013089955625320671,"t":"which Barr discusses the impact of climate change on the city and makes policy suggestions for"},{"c":1,"w":0.19958848317464195,"y":0.84447674403395478,"h":0.01598837238037687,"t":"New York going forward.","x":0.11728394761020988},{"c":1,"y":0.86773255796252025,"h":0.013081395583944722,"x":0.11522633697284339,"w":0.026748971939086916,"t":"68"},{"x":0.6698449616748573,"t":"IELTSXpress.com","w":0.30612408955891923,"y":0.95314993666560754,"h":0.035150757105527397,"c":1}]}